==============================================================================
 PHASE 2 NOTES - READING THE CODE (AST METRICS AND COMPLEXITY)
==============================================================================

Goal of Phase 2:
  Phase 1 looked at files from the OUTSIDE (name, size, lines).
  Phase 2 OPENS each .java file and understands the code inside:
  which classes and methods exist, and how complicated each method is.

Slices (from the playbook):
  2A  Parse one file: PARSED or PARSE_FAILED                   <- DONE
  2B  Find declarations: classes, interfaces, records, methods...  <- DONE
  2C  Token-based source lines (ncloc) and comment lines
  2D  Comments: TODO / FIXME markers
  2E  Method line ranges
  2F  Complexity (the 5-point example)


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA - THREE WAYS TO SEE CODE: TEXT, TOKENS, TREE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Take this one line:   if (x > 10) say();

1. TEXT   = just characters.  'i' 'f' ' ' '(' 'x' ...
            A computer sees letters. It does not know what "if" means.

2. TOKENS = the text chopped into WORDS.
            [if] [(] [x] [>] [10] [)] [say] [(] [)] [;]
            Like splitting an English sentence into words.

3. TREE (AST = Abstract Syntax Tree) = the words arranged by MEANING.

            IfStmt
            |-- condition:  BinaryExpr  ( x  >  10 )
            |-- thenStmt:   MethodCall  say()

            Like a sentence diagram from grammar class:
            subject, verb, object.

Why do we need the tree?
  To count methods, we need to know "this is a method". A text search
  for "void" would be fooled by a comment like  // void of meaning
  or a string like "void". The tree is never fooled: it knows exactly
  which part is a method, which is a comment, which is a string.

See a real tree yourself:  (commands at the bottom of this file)
  ParseMain SourceWorkspace.java --tree
  shows CompilationUnit -> packageDeclaration -> imports -> types ->
  ClassOrInterfaceDeclaration (isInterface: true) -> MethodDeclaration "root"


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA - PARSING IS NOT COMPILING (safe vs dangerous)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Parsing   = READ the code and build the tree.   Like reading a recipe.
  Compiling = turn it into bytecode (javac).      Like preparing to cook.
  Running   = execute it (java).                  Like cooking and eating.

CodePulse only PARSES. It never compiles or runs analyzed code.
Why? The code comes from strangers on GitHub. Running it could delete
files or steal secrets. Reading a recipe cannot burn your kitchen.

Parsing also needs LESS than compiling: the parser does not need the
other files or libraries the code uses. It checks grammar, not meaning.
  "int x = hello;"  parses fine (grammar OK) even if "hello" does not
  exist. A compiler would reject it. A parser does not care.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 SLICE 2A - NEW IDEAS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. A LIBRARY (dependency): JavaParser 3.28.2
   Added to pom.xml. Maven downloads it into ~/.m2 once.
   We pin the exact version so everybody gets identical results.
   The spec (API contract) also records "parser": "3.28.2" in reports.

2. PARSED vs PARSE_FAILED
   Broken code (missing "}") -> PARSE_FAILED.
   SURPRISE we found by experiment: JavaParser still returns a
   half-built "recovered" tree for broken code. We IGNORE it.
   Half a tree = half-true numbers. CodePulse never guesses.

3. Java 21 WITHOUT previews
   "Preview" features are experiments that Java may still change.
   Our promise is Java 21 final features only.
   - "int _ = 1;"          (preview in 21) -> JavaParser rejects it
   - "void main() {...}"   alone in a file (preview in 21) -> JavaParser
                           ACCEPTS it! So we added our own check
                           (isCompact) -> UNSUPPORTED_SYNTAX.
   Lesson: do not assume a library does what you need. Test it.

4. Diagnostics WITHOUT source text (security)
   JavaParser's error message can quote the code, e.g. a variable
   called passwordValue. We keep ONLY:
       code (SYNTAX_ERROR / UNSUPPORTED_SYNTAX), line, column
   A test checks that "passwordValue" never appears in a diagnostic.
   We keep at most 10 diagnostics per file, but count all of them.

5. Optional = a box that may be empty.
     outcome.syntaxTree()  returns Optional<CompilationUnit>
     PARSED       -> box has the tree
     PARSE_FAILED -> box is empty
   Instead of null (which you can forget to check), Optional FORCES
   you to think "what if it is empty?".
     tree.ifPresentOrElse(t -> print(t), () -> print("no tree"));
   Chaining:  a.flatMap(...).map(...)  keeps going only while there
   is a value. If any step is empty, the result is empty. We use this
   to get an error's line number, which JavaParser does not always have.
   Unknown line -> null (unknown), never 0.

6. Why ParseOutcome is a CLASS, not a RECORD
   A record makes every field public through an accessor. That would
   expose a tree field that is null for failed parses. As a class,
   the ONLY way to reach the tree is syntaxTree(), which returns
   Optional. The design makes the mistake impossible.

7. No global parser
   We create our own JavaParser object with fixed settings (Java 21,
   UTF-8), instead of the global StaticJavaParser. Global settings can
   be changed by anyone, anywhere; our own object cannot.

8. Test fixtures in src/test/resources
   Fixture files like MissingBrace.java are plain TEXT that contain
   Java. Maven copies resources without compiling them, so broken
   fixtures do not break our build. Tests load them from the
   classpath with getResourceAsStream.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 DEBUGGING / REVIEW LESSON FROM 2A (real)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The first version of the "cap at 10 diagnostics" test passed, but it
was WEAK. Running the input by hand showed it produced only 1 problem,
so the cap was never reached. A test that cannot fail proves nothing.
Fix: 15 "int _" lines -> exactly 15 problems -> test checks 15 counted,
10 kept, first at line 3 col 9, last kept at line 12 col 9.
Lesson: after a test passes, ask "would this test FAIL if the code
were wrong?"


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HOW TO TEST SLICE 2A, STEP BY STEP
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 1  cd ~/Desktop/CodePulse/backend
STEP 2  ./mvnw test
        Expect: Tests run: 39, Failures: 0, Errors: 0   BUILD SUCCESS
        (JavaSourceParserTest: 7 tests)

STEP 3  JavaParser is now a library, so "java -cp target/classes" is not
        enough. Ask Maven for the full list of jars once:
          ./mvnw -q dependency:build-classpath -Dmdep.outputFile=target/classpath.txt
        Then:
          CP="target/classes:$(cat target/classpath.txt)"

STEP 4  Parse a broken file:
          java -cp "$CP" dev.codepulse.ParseMain src/test/resources/fixtures/parse/MissingBrace.java
        Output:
          status: PARSE_FAILED
          problems: 1
            SYNTAX_ERROR at line 8, column 5
        Meaning: line 8 is the end of the file; the parser reached the end
        still waiting for "}".

STEP 5  Parse a preview feature:
          java -cp "$CP" dev.codepulse.ParseMain src/test/resources/fixtures/parse/UnnamedClass.java
        Output: status: PARSE_FAILED, UNSUPPORTED_SYNTAX at line 3, column 1

STEP 6  SEE A TREE (best learning step):
          java -cp "$CP" dev.codepulse.ParseMain src/main/java/dev/codepulse/engine/SourceWorkspace.java --tree
        Find: CompilationUnit, packageDeclaration, imports,
        ClassOrInterfaceDeclaration (isInterface: "true"),
        MethodDeclaration with name "root".
        Try it on Phase0Main.java too, and find the IfStmt.


==============================================================================
 SLICE 2B - COUNTING DECLARATIONS WITH A VISITOR
==============================================================================

What it does:
  For a PARSED file, count what is written in it:
    class, interface, enum, record, annotation, anonymous class,
    method, constructor, executable, lambda
  A broken (PARSE_FAILED) file gets NO counts at all.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA - THE VISITOR (a tour guide walking the tree)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The tree can be deep: a class inside a class inside a method...
We need to visit EVERY node exactly once.

JavaParser gives us VoidVisitorAdapter: a tour guide that already
knows how to walk every room (node) and every room inside it.
We only say what to do in the rooms we care about:

    @Override
    public void visit(MethodDeclaration node, Void arg) {
        methodCount++;                 // 1. do our job in this room
        super.visit(node, arg);        // 2. "guide, continue into the rooms inside"
    }

RECURSION: the guide enters a room, and for each room inside it,
does the same thing again. That is why nested classes work with no
extra code from us.

THE CLASSIC BUG - forgetting super.visit(...)
  Without it, the guide stops at that room and never goes inside.
  We TRIED this on purpose (real experiment):
    removed super.visit in the class visitor ->
    expected method 10 ... but was method 0
    3 tests failed. Then we put the line back: 45/45 pass.
  So our tests would catch this bug if anyone made it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BIG IDEA - EXPLICIT vs IMPLICIT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Java secretly writes some code for you ("implicit"):
    record Point(int x, int y) {}   -> constructor, x(), y(), equals...
    enum Color { RED, GREEN }       -> values(), valueOf()
    class Empty {}                  -> a default constructor

CodePulse counts only what the PROGRAMMER WROTE ("explicit").
For the three lines above: methods 0, constructors 0.
Why? We measure the code people must read and review. Hidden
generated code is not something a reviewer reads.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 THE COUNTING RULES (Blueprint 9.3), with examples
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  class        named classes, INCLUDING nested and local ones
               NOT interfaces, NOT anonymous "new X() { }"
  interface    "interface Greeter" (an @interface is NOT an interface here)
  annotation   "@interface Audit" - its "String value();" is NOT a method
  anonymous    "new Runnable() { ... }" - counted separately, not a class
  method       every written method, INCLUDING abstract ones with no body
  constructor  written constructors, INCLUDING record compact ones: Item { ... }
  executable   methods and constructors that HAVE A BODY { ... }
                  "abstract int weight();"   method yes, executable NO
                  "default String greet() {}" method yes, executable YES
  lambda       "name -> ..." counted, but NOT an executable

The big fixture (Declarations.java) has a running tally written in
comments next to each line. Open it and follow along:
  class 2, interface 1, enum 1, record 1, annotation 1, anonymous 1,
  method 10, constructor 3, executable 11, lambda 2

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 TEST-FIRST (red -> green), real run
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Wrote the fixture and counted by hand FIRST.
2. Wrote the tests with those hand numbers.
3. Ran them against an empty "stub" -> RED: 5 ERRORS.
   (Errors, not Failures: the stub CRASHED before comparing numbers.
    Remember Phase 0: Failure = wrong answer, Error = crash.)
4. Wrote the real visitor -> GREEN: all pass.
Why this order? The numbers come from YOUR head, not from the code.
If we ran the code first and copied its output, a bug would become
the "expected" answer.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 BROKEN FILE = NO NUMBERS (the half-tree lesson, made concrete)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    class Half { void one() {} void two() {
The tree stops early. A half-tree might say "1 method" - wrong, but
it LOOKS real. So CodePulse gives NO counts:
    outcome.syntaxTree().map(DeclarationCounter::countIn)  -> empty
Test: aBrokenFileGetsNoCountsAtAllNotSmallerCounts

